# Licensing

Woody is released under three licences, one per kind of work. All three are
**share-alike**: derivatives stay open on the same terms.

| Path | Licence | SPDX |
|---|---|---|
| `firmware/`, `tools/` | GNU General Public License v3.0 only | `GPL-3.0-only` |
| `hardware/`, `mechanical/` | CERN Open Hardware Licence v2 — Strongly Reciprocal | `CERN-OHL-S-2.0` |
| `docs/`, `README.md`, `ROADMAP.md` | Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International | `CC-BY-SA-4.0` |
| `config/` | Either of `GPL-3.0-only` **or** `CERN-OHL-S-2.0`, at your option | see below |

Full texts are in [`LICENSES/`](LICENSES/).

## Why three

They cover different kinds of work and the wrong one is unenforceable or
meaningless against the others. The GPL's "source code" and "linking" have no
sensible reading against a DXF or a bill of materials; CERN-OHL's "Available
Component" and "Product" have none against a firmware image. Using one licence
for everything would mean using one that does not fit two thirds of the
repository.

The choices match the project's history and its neighbours: GPLv3 continues
[the 2021 Open Woodwind Project](https://github.com/jeffmhopkins/Open-Woodwind-Project),
CERN-OHL-S is the prevailing licence for open hardware of this kind, and
CC-BY-SA is the normal choice for documentation.

## `config/` is deliberately dual-licensed

`config/key-layout.yaml` is the single source of truth for both the firmware's
key-to-bit mapping and the DXF that cuts the aluminium plate (ADR 0010). It is
genuinely both a source file and a hardware design document, so it carries both
licences and you may use it under either.

This is not a technicality. The whole point of that file is that one artefact
feeds both sides, and a licence boundary drawn through the middle of it would
recreate exactly the divergence it exists to prevent.

